.TH OOBED 8 "September 2026" "Peios" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
oobed \- Peios first-boot setup service
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B oobed
.RB [ --socket " " PATH ]
.RB [ --browser " " PATH " | " --no-browser ]
.RB [ --dry-run
.RB [ --net-status " " FILE ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B oobed
owns the privileged first-boot conversation.  It creates the first
administrator account, records the machine name in the registry, and retires
its own service definitions only after setup completes.
.PP
The terminal presentation is provided by
.BR oobe-tui (1)
so that user-interface code does not own account or registry privilege.
.PP
The network page says what
.B net status
prints of the machine's network, an interface to a line, and checks again when
asked; it changes nothing, and nothing on it has to be answered.
.PP
Where the browser program is installed, setup can also be done in a browser,
as the GXWI overlay: before it accepts connections,
.B oobed
creates the passwordless account
.B peios-oobe-setup
(or keeps it, from an earlier boot whose setup did not finish), admits it to
its socket, and sets
.I OverlayUsername
and
.I OverlaySession
under
.IR Machine\eSoftware\eGXWI .
Both surfaces draw the same conversation.  When setup completes it removes
the two values, the account and its home directory before retiring.  The
account page refuses the name
.BR peios-oobe-setup .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --socket " PATH"
Listen on PATH instead of
.IR /run/oobed.sock .
.TP
.BI --browser " PATH"
The program GXWI runs to draw setup in a browser.  The default is
.IR /bin/oobe-gxwi ;
if it is not installed, setup is on the console only.
.TP
.B --no-browser
Setup on the console only.
.TP
.B --dry-run
Exercise the setup conversation without changing the machine.  No account is
created and no overlay is set, so a browser program is pointed at the socket
by hand.
.TP
.BI --net-status " FILE"
With
.BR --dry-run ,
take FILE to be what
.B net status
prints, read afresh each time the network page asks.  Without it, a dry run
cannot say anything of the network.
.TP
.BR -h , " --help"
Show command help.
.TP
.BR -V , " --version"
Show the program version.
.SH SECURITY
The service runs as SYSTEM and accepts MSIP connections only from SYSTEM,
Administrators and, while setup is offered in a browser,
.BR peios-oobe-setup .
That account has no password, so while it exists anything that logs on
without a credential can log on as it; it holds no groups, and what it can
reach is this socket, which the browser offers anyone who can open the
machine's address.  Passwords are sent to
.B lps
over standard input, never as process arguments, and are not written to the
MSIP log.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/oobed.sock
Default MSIP socket.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR oobe-tui (1),
.BR lps (1),
.BR net (1),
.BR reg (1)
